*Current system and decision boundary*
#v(0.28em)
This chapter turns a request into a reviewable change. A change must have an owner, affected components, security impact, test evidence, a release note, and a rollback decision before it is treated as complete.
#v(0.35em)
*Concrete implementation locations*
#v(0.22em)
#text(font: "DejaVu Sans Mono", size: 7.0pt)[GitHub main · pull request review · implementation tracker · git tags · release notes]
],
[
*What to do and how to do it*
#v(0.28em)
1. Create a small work item with acceptance criteria, security impact, test plan, and rollback decision. Branch from the reviewed main revision and keep the branch limited to one coherent problem.
#v(0.25em)
2. Make the change with a clear commit sequence. For database work, commit the new migration and application callers together; never rewrite an applied migration to make local history look cleaner.
#v(0.25em)
3. Ask a second person to review the affected route, RLS policy, migration, and private-data behavior. Reviewers should reproduce the user journey rather than approving only a code diff.
#v(0.25em)
4. Merge only when tests, production build, migration plan, release note, and rollback path are recorded. If a release fails, return to the known commit rather than making undocumented live edits.
])
