*Current system and decision boundary*
#v(0.28em)
This chapter gives concrete steps for growth, backup, restore testing, and provider migration. Supabase is an integrated Auth, database, RLS, Storage, realtime, and RPC platform, so moving away requires replacing all of those responsibilities.
#v(0.35em)
*Concrete implementation locations*
#v(0.22em)
#text(font: "DejaVu Sans Mono", size: 7.0pt)[Team_Handover_2026/03_SCALING_AND_MIGRATION_RUNBOOK.md · Supabase project · Storage manifests · DNS records · target infrastructure]
],
[
*What to do and how to do it*
#v(0.28em)
1. Define the growth threshold using real metrics: active users, messages, posts, Storage objects, query latency, support queue volume, and deployment/cold-start behavior. Do not design a million-user architecture without a measured trigger.
#v(0.25em)
2. Create separate source, schema, data, and Storage backups with timestamp, checksum, object manifest, source commit, and retention rule. Restore a representative backup into an isolated environment on a documented schedule.
#v(0.25em)
3. For Render migration, reproduce environment variables in a target secret manager, deploy the same commit to staging, test OAuth/origins, lower DNS TTL, switch only after end-to-end checks, and retain a rollback domain path.
#v(0.25em)
4. For Supabase migration, replace Postgres, Auth, RLS authorization, Storage signing, RPC behavior, realtime, and notification responsibilities one by one. Rehearse import, compare row/object counts and critical flows, then conduct a timed cutover with rollback.
])
